{"cells":[{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"b6441bd7","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.01354,"end_time":"2026-11-07T07:11:34.419637","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-07T07:11:34.406097","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["\n","# V3 Student-Attention Federated Learning Benchmark — Kaggle Notebook\n","\n","This notebook is a V3-specific redesign of the earlier benchmark.\n","\n","The benchmark is centered on six questions:\n","\n","1. **Benchmark validity:** how readily can the released behavioral features reconstruct the released attention label?\n","2. **Feature dependence:** which V3 feature groups contribute most to that operational label?\n","3. **Federated heterogeneity:** how does FL behave as label heterogeneity increases, and what happens under feature-cluster heterogeneity?\n","4. **Privacy–utility:** what happens under record-level DP-SGD with explicit cumulative RDP accounting?\n","5. **Client unreliability:** how do FedAvg, FedProx, coordinate-median, trimmed-mean, and CAFAD behave under poisoning/straggler conditions?\n","6. **Evaluation-time robustness:** how sensitive are the learned global models to FGSM perturbations?\n","\n","### Scientific scope\n","\n","- FL clients are **simulated partitions of the training records**, not identified real students or real devices.\n","- The public V3 release does not expose a subject/session identifier, so this notebook does **not** claim subject-disjoint generalization or student-level DP.\n","- DP results are scoped to the **record/row level**.\n","- CAFAD uses a client-side validation score. For the formal DP study, CAFAD is excluded unless that validation statistic is separately privatized.\n","- The target is the released label (`0 = attentive`, `1 = inattentive`). The feature-dependence section tests how much of that label is reconstructable from the released behavioral signals.\n"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"4b2d976f","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.010332,"end_time":"2026-11-07T07:11:34.440417","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-07T07:11:34.430085","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["## Archive audit and required interpretation (2026-10-10)\n","\n","The attached previous-results archive was checked before updating this notebook.\n","\n","- The archived clean FL file has 600 rows with no duplicate `(seed, condition, alpha, method)` keys; the corrected robustness file has 300 rows with 20 seeds per scenario-method combination.\n","- The archive's `dp_privacy_utility_v3.csv` has 300 rows (240 finite-epsilon runs and 60 `epsilon=inf` rows), but it **does not contain** `dp_protocol_version`, `dp_rng_mode`, or `nonprivate_control_protocol` fields. It therefore cannot establish that it used the corrected DP-SGD mechanism or matched non-private controls. Its DP metrics and manuscript DP table are historical only; do not cite them as formal DP results.\n","- The archived client-count sensitivity has 180 rows including K=20, alpha=0.1 partitions that required post-hoc minimum-size repair. The updated notebook excludes alpha=0.1 from the client-count sensitivity comparison and reports only IID and Dirichlet alpha=0.5, with K=10 reused from the primary benchmark.\n","- The feature-group audit supports a benchmark-validity concern: the shallow tree using four direct cues averages about 92.36% accuracy, nearly the same as all 37 predictors (about 92.31%). Interpret this as strong feature–label construction dependence, not evidence of subject-independent generalization.\n","\n","Run the corrected DP cells to produce the v2 DP CSV before regenerating the manuscript tables. The notebook deliberately fails closed if `torchcsprng` is unavailable for a formal DP run. Install a compatible version, restart the kernel, and rerun setup/imports and the DP definition cells before proceeding.\n"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":1,"id":"a2a1d54f","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-07T07:11:34.462715Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-1